Project 5

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description:

This project simulates resource management in an operating system, including process creation, resource allocation, blocking, and deadlock detection.

The oss program acts as the operating system. It maintains a simulated system clock in shared memory using seconds and nanoseconds. It also manages a process table of up to 20 process control blocks (PCBs), tracking process state, allocated resources, and pending requests.

Processes communicate with oss using System V message queues. Each worker randomly requests or releases resources during execution.

If a resource request cannot be granted, the process becomes blocked and is placed in a blocked queue until the resource becomes available.

The system periodically runs a deadlock detection algorithm. If a deadlock involving multiple processes is detected, oss resolves it by terminating one of the deadlocked processes and releasing its resources.

Additionally, blocked processes that exceed their maximum lifetime are terminated by oss to prevent indefinite waiting.

The simulation prints the process table and resource allocation state at regular intervals and terminates after all processes complete or a time limit is 
reached.

Worker processes simulate resource usage by randomly requesting or releasing resources. Each request is sent to oss, which either grants it immediately or blocks the process if the resource is unavailable.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ 

Deadlock Handling:

The system periodically checks for deadlock by simulating whether blocked processes can eventually proceed given available resources.

A deadlock is only considered valid if two or more processes are blocked. If deadlock is detected, oss selects one process to terminate, releases its resources, and allows the remaining processes to continue.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

How to compile:

Type 'make'

This will compile both oss and worker exec. 

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Example of how to run:


./oss [-h] [-n proc] [-s simul] [-t timeLimitForChildren] [-i intervalToLaunch] [-f logfile]

  -h       Show help message and exit
  -n proc  Total number of worker processes to launch (max 80)
  -s simul Maximum number of workers allowed to run simultaneously (max 15)
  -t secs  Simulated time limit passed to each worker (float, e.g. 2.5)
  -i secs  Minimum simulated interval between launches (float, e.g. 0.1)
  -f file  Log file name for scheduler output 
Example:
  ./oss -n 5 -s 3 -t 4 -i 0.5 -f log.txt

This launches 5 workers total, no more than 3 at a time, each running for
up to 4 simulated seconds, with at least 0.5 simulated seconds between
each launch and the output is written to log.txt.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Problems Encountered:


- Debugging timing behavior was difficult because the simulated clock only advances when work is done, rather than continuously.

- Any nanosecond value exceeding 1,000,000,000 required conversion into seconds.

- Added a safeguard to terminate blocked processes whose simulated runtime has expired. Without this, a single blocked process could wait indefinitely if no 
  other process releases the requested resource. 

- Proper cleanup of shared memory and message queues to prevent resource leaks when the program terminated unexpectedly. Using the command below to mannually cleanup:

ipcs
ipcrm -m <shmid>
ipcrm -q <msgid>

